    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d176">

                <pb facs="0496" n="453" xml:id="pg453"/>

                <head>176. Transcript of a Telephone Conversation Between President Nixon and the President’s Assistant for
                    National Security Affairs (Kissinger)<anchor n="1" xml:id="d176-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1970-11-09T15:40:00-05:00">November 9, 1970,
                            3:40 p.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>[Omitted here is discussion unrelated to Chile.]</p>
                <p>[P:] Anything new on Chile?</p>
                <p>K: We have put out directives on Friday.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d176-f2"/> Their
                    propaganda is stepping up against us but nothing overt.</p>
                <p>P: After making the statement he did<anchor n="3" xml:id="d176-f3"/> [omission is
                    in the original] it’s as cold as that. Helms has to get to these people. Get out of it now.</p>
                <p>K: We have made that clear.</p>
                <p>P: Re-scheduling of debts comes up when?</p>
                <p>K: Early next year. We have put in the instructions at the NSC meeting into the directive. I am holding a
                    meeting on Chile in the Sr. Review Group every 3 weeks.</p>
                <p>P: I feel strongly this line is important regarding its effect on the people of
                    the world. If he can prove he can set up a Marxist anti-American policy, others
                    will do the same thing.</p>
                <p>K: It will haveeffect even in Europe. Not only Latin America.</p>
                <p>[Omitted here is discussion unrelated to Chile.]</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
